  "nodes": [
    {
      "height": 60,
      "id": "1c93a7bf4e7999c8",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[iTriangle - The Beginning - T1 - Genesis 1 . 1 to John 1 . 1#OT Text|OT Text]]",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 260,
      "x": -620,
      "y": 320
    },
    {
      "height": 200,
      "id": "90e64ac5f8149757",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[iTriangle - The Beginning - T1 - Genesis 1 . 1 to John 1 . 1#Fulfillment in Christ|Fulfillment in Christ]] -  it assumes that the interpreter understands the message of the text in the contexts of the whole canon (including the New Testament) and all of redemptive history",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 260,
      "x": 800,
      "y": -370
    },
    {
      "height": 60,
      "id": "0ddfe4370eb3cad3",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[iTriangle - The Beginning - T1 - Genesis 1 . 1 to John 1 . 1#Significance|Significance]]",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 260,
      "x": 800,
      "y": 10
    },
    {
      "height": 60,
      "id": "1f2bbd0e10d03864",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[iTriangle - The Beginning - T1 - Genesis 1 . 1 to John 1 . 1#Application (ACtS)|Application: ACtS]]",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 260,
      "x": 800,
      "y": 320
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 400,
      "id": "7e341c6a2272fb01",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Promise Fulfillment - A more direct road to Christ from an Old Testament text is the way of promise-fulfillment. If the text contains a promise of the coming Messiah, the preacher can move to the New Testament to show the ultimate fulfillment of that promise in Jesus Christ.",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 210,
      "x": -890,
      "y": 620
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 400,
      "id": "a766f9625e8a90a3",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Contrast - A final road from the Old Testament to Christ is the way of contrast. Because of the coming of Christ the text’s message for the contemporary church may be quite different from the original message for Israel. For example, Circumcision as a sign of covenant membership (Genesis 17:11) is contrasted with Baptism (Colossians 2:11-14)",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 240,
      "x": 980,
      "y": 620
    },
    {
      "height": 60,
      "id": "f5c294ad7f0efd9c",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[iMap - The Beginning|History of Revelation]] ",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 260,
      "x": 40,
      "y": -300
    },
    {
      "height": 100,
      "id": "43c9e32fb82c65f2",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[01 - Issues in Preaching Christ from Genesis|Sidney Greidanus - Preaching Christ from Genesis: Foundations for Expository Sermons]]",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 310,
      "x": 140,
      "y": 1120
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 400,
      "id": "af87fb3721e1233d",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Typology - Another road from an Old Testament text to Christ is typology. Old Testament redemptive events, persons, or institutions can function as types which foreshadow the great Antitype, the person and/or work of Jesus Christ.",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 200,
      "x": -640,
      "y": 620
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 400,
      "id": "a4897ecebfc013bf",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Analogy - A fourth and more general road from an Old Testament text to Christ is the way of analogy. Analogy exposes parallels between what God taught Israel and what Christ teaches the church; what God promised Israel and what Christ promises the church; what God demanded of Israel (the law) and what Christ demands of his church.",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 240,
      "x": -400,
      "y": 620
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 400,
      "id": "62aeedb9dee3577c",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Longitudinal Themes - Another road from an Old Testament text to Christ is the way of longitudinal themes. It focuses on the development of theological ideas rather than development in redemptive history.  It refers to themes that can be traced through the Scriptures from the Old Testament to the New—themes such as God’s coming kingdom, God’s covenant, God’s redemption, God’s presence, God’s love, God’s faithfulness, God’s grace, God’s judgment, God’s providence.",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 300,
      "x": -120,
      "y": 620
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 400,
      "id": "b26de36a77fb5081",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "New Testament References - Another road from an Old Testament text to Christ is that of New Testament references. Sometimes the New Testament alludes to or quotes the selected preaching-text and links it to Christ. In this case, the New Testament citation may possibly serve as a bridge to Christ.",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 220,
      "x": 710,
      "y": 620
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 400,
      "id": "9d35dac5d6d590ba",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Redemptive-Historical Progression - - The broadest and foundational path from an Old Testament text to Jesus Christ is the way of redemptive-historical progression. It traces God’s history with the world from his good creation (Gen 1), to the human fall into sin and God’s plan of redemption through the seed of the woman (Gen 3:15), to a long history of God continuing the line of the seed of the woman (Gen 3–Malachi), to Christ (the Gospels), the church (Acts and New Testament Letters), and finally to the new creation (Rev 22). In other words, in the Bible we can trace a continuous redemptive history which centers in the life, death, and resurrection of Jesus Christ, who then ascends to rule his church from heaven until he comes again",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 420,
      "x": 240,
      "y": 620
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 90,
      "id": "c9a8cb2e8487c6da",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Longitudinal Themes\nRedemptive-Historical Progression",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 300,
      "x": 40,
      "y": -460
    },
    {
      "color": "1",
      "height": 160,
      "id": "43fa7f678de1ff97",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "Promise Fulfillment \nLine of Typology \nAnalogy \nNew Testament References\nContrast",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 60,
      "y": -40
    },
    {
      "height": 110,
      "id": "be243fa186e69b04",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[iTriangle - The Beginning - T1 - Genesis 1 . 1 to John 1 . 1#Context|Context]] - interpreter understands the message of the text in its own historical context",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 260,
      "x": -620,
      "y": -15
    },
    {
      "height": 120,
      "id": "816832454592826f",
      "styleAttributes": {
      },
      "text": "[[iTriangle - The Beginning - T1 - Genesis 1 . 1 to John 1 . 1#Original Intent|Original Intent]] - the interpreter seeks to discern the message the writer wanted Israel to hear",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 260,
      "x": -620,
      "y": -330
    }
  ]
}